---
// R1–R3: the values that put the domain back.
//
// In the page rather than only in a runbook document on purpose. The operator
// note on S9 says these must ALSO live somewhere reachable without a checkout,
// because a rollback procedure only its author can open is not a rollback
// procedure — but the version here is what someone reads while deciding whether
// to roll back at all.
import NumberedLine from './NumberedLine.astro';
---
<section id="rollback">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>If something's wrong</h2>
    <div class="lede">Putting it back is three values, and they're written here on purpose.</div>
    <p class="sub">
      Rollback works because Strikingly stays alive until S9 and because the TTLs were
      lowered in S7 — so reverting propagates in minutes, the same as the switch did.
      These are the values as of the 2026-07-02 discovery; S1 re-confirms them, and
      whatever S1 finds is what belongs here.
    </p>
    <ul class="lines danger">
      <NumberedLine id="R1">Apex <code>harvardintech.com</code> → <code>A 54.183.102.22</code> (Strikingly). Remove the four GitHub <code>A</code> records.</NumberedLine>
      <NumberedLine id="R2"><code>www</code> → <code>CNAME www.harvardintech.com.s.strikinglydns.com</code>.</NumberedLine>
      <NumberedLine id="R3">Leave <code>MX</code>, <code>mail.</code>, and <code>TXT</code> alone — as in the cutover, so they are alone in the rollback. If email <em>has</em> broken, these records are where to look, and the fix is restoring exactly what S1 captured.</NumberedLine>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
